CITYKART.SHOP — EXHAUSTIVE FULL-APPLICATION, FIREBASE BACKEND AND PRODUCTION-READINESS AUDIT

ROLE AND OBJECTIVE

Act as a senior software architect, Firebase security specialist, full-stack engineer, application security auditor, QA automation engineer and production-readiness reviewer.

Conduct a comprehensive, evidence-based audit of the entire existing Citykart.shop web application and its Firebase backend. Inspect the actual codebase and configuration, verify the integration between the frontend and backend, identify defects and security vulnerabilities, execute available tests, and safely fix verified issues.

The objective is to establish whether the existing application is genuinely ready for production use, with particular emphasis on Firebase configuration, data integrity, authentication, authorization, order processing, financial calculations, subscription billing and Razorpay integration.

Do not merely review the homepage or perform a superficial code scan. Audit the complete application, including all routes, features, backend services, database collections, security rules, cloud functions, integrations and deployment configuration that can be discovered in the project.

This is an existing application. Preserve its architecture, business rules, branding, production data and working functionality wherever possible.

---

PHASE 1 — DISCOVER THE COMPLETE APPLICATION

1.1 Inspect the repository

Before modifying anything, inspect the complete project structure.

Identify and document:

- Frontend framework, version and build system.
- Application entry points and route definitions.
- All public pages and authenticated pages.
- Buyer, seller, administrator and super-administrator interfaces.
- Shared components, forms, state management and data-access layers.
- Authentication providers and session handling.
- Firebase initialization and environment configuration.
- Firestore collections, document models and query patterns.
- Firebase Storage usage and upload workflows.
- Cloud Functions, scheduled jobs and event-driven functions.
- Firebase Hosting configuration and hosting targets.
- Firebase App Check configuration, if present.
- Razorpay payment and subscription integration.
- External APIs, notification services and other third-party integrations.
- GitHub Actions or other CI/CD workflows.
- Environment variables, secrets references and configuration files.
- Existing tests, lint rules, type checking and build scripts.

Inspect the actual files rather than assuming that a documented feature is implemented.

Create a complete feature inventory and map each feature to its frontend components, backend operations, database dependencies and authorization requirements.

1.2 Establish a baseline

Before making changes:

1. Inspect the current Git working tree and existing user changes.
2. Record the current branch and recent relevant changes where available.
3. Run the available production build.
4. Run available lint, type-check and automated test commands.
5. Record existing errors, warnings and failures.
6. Identify whether failures existed before the audit.
7. Check for incomplete features, TODO markers, mock implementations, hardcoded data and placeholder integrations.

Do not overwrite uncommitted changes or revert unrelated work. Never expose secret values in logs or the audit report.

